+### Merging duplicate tickets
+
+Staff can merge a duplicate into a different, nonclosed ticket for the same customer
+in the same account:
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/tickets/42/merge
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{"merge":{"target_id":57}}
+```
+
+Returns `200 {"source":{…},"target":{…}}` using staff ticket representations.
+The source becomes `closed` with `merged_into_id: 57`; the target's status,
+priority, assignment, subject, and email identity are unchanged. Old comments stay
+on their original tickets. Both tickets receive a private note explaining the merge,
+authored by the staff member. Optional note bodies and visibility can be set independently:
+
+```json
+{"merge":{"target_id":57,"source_comment":{"body":"Continuing in ticket 57.","public":true},"target_comment":{"body":"Duplicate checked.","public":false}}}
+```
+
+Omitted bodies use the default explanation; omitted visibility defaults to private.
+Merge notes never send email, even when public. Customers see only public notes,
+but can always read their closed source and follow `merged_into_id`. Both list
+endpoints accept `status=closed`. Closed sources reject edits, comments, and email
+replies (replies are not redirected to the target). Internal assignment cleanup on
+suspension, membership removal, or group deletion still applies.
+
+Self-merges, different-customer merges, and merges with either ticket already closed
+return `422`. Missing or other-account tickets return `404`; customers cannot merge
+(`403`). `target_id` must be a positive JSON integer, note bodies must be strings,
+and visibility must be a JSON boolean (`400` for malformed input). Blank note bodies
+return `422`. The operation is atomic: validation failure leaves both tickets and
+comments unchanged. If ticket 57 is later merged into ticket 63, ticket 42 still points
+to 57, preserving the original history rather than flattening the chain.
+
